DepPriority: copy priorities slot by slot, build them from templates

Sub-series: J (priority copy) - patch 1 of 1; applies to 3.0.83 with only the earlier patches of this sub-series.
Unchanged, but the patch before it is now required-use-memo (probe-certain-atoms, the old 0022, is 0029); the step figures below are those of the old order.
Ported to upstream 73646c35d: unchanged (line numbers only)

A priority object is created or copied for every dependency.
AbstractDepPriority.copy() does what copy.copy() does for a slots-only
class without the __reduce_ex__ detour, DepPriority.copy() assigns its ten
slots directly, and depgraph._priority() keeps one template per
combination of flag arguments (True/False/None only) and returns copies.

On top of patches 0001-0022, resolve seconds before -> after
(median of the runs, at least 2 per figure):
  start 23.0 -> 22.1, holds 11.8 -> 11.5, masks 24.1 -> 23.4,
  py313 14.4 -> 14.0, bdeps 16.9 -> 16.2, policykit 12.0 -> 11.7
  sum of six 102.2 -> 98.8 (-3.4 s, -0.8 % of the stock sum 447.5)
Patches 0001-0023 together: 447.5 -> 98.8 s (-78 %); 447.5 is the
stock sum of the ladder run of 2026-11-20, stock was not run again here.

Kept as its own small patch: it is a different mechanism from 0017 (copying,
not constructing), and in the second order that was measured (chain-
prelim2), where it followed 0017 directly, its step was about 5 s.

Weak spots and what was not checked:
* Uses copyreg._slotnames(), a private helper of the standard library.

Origin: written by AI agents (Claude) in the emerge-fast job, 2026-11-17..20;
isolated from the full patch and measured on 2026-11-20..21. Not a human-written
change. Base: Portage 3.0.83. Test: `emerge -p -uND @world` on six states of
one system image, one resolve at a time; every run gave the same merge list
in the same order, the same number of backtracking attempts and the same
package count as unpatched 3.0.83.
